<!--
  UNTITLED FIGHTING GAME — the title's own front end.

  ===========================================================================
  THIS FILE IS THE POINT OF THE CONTENT SEPARATION
  ===========================================================================
  Until this existed, the fighting game was ONE MORE VERB on the engine's demo
  menu: a button in Editor/src/Exported/UI/menu.cxml, on a scene the engine
  owns, beside a NEW GAME that loads a room with a backpack in it. The engine's
  menu is a good menu and it is not this game's menu — nobody sells a game whose
  title screen belongs to the toolkit it was built with.

  So the game owns the whole front end. Nothing here is in the engine's asset
  root: this file, its stylesheet, its art and the scene that carries it all
  live under Games/UntitledFighter/Assets/ and are LAYERED into the runtime
  Exported/ by cmake/stage_runtime_assets.cmake. Delete the title's
  add_subdirectory from the root CMakeLists and every one of them is pruned out
  of the staged tree again, and the player boots the engine demo exactly as it
  did before — which is the property the boundary assertion exists to protect.

  Nothing INSTALLS this file. Games/UntitledFighter/Assets/UntitledFighter/
  menu.json has an entity carrying a UIDocumentComponent that points at it, and
  the host boots that scene because MyCoreEngine::TitleFrontEndScene() — the
  seam in Engine/src/core/TitleFrontEnd.h, defined in
  ../../../Player/src/TitleFrontEnd.cpp — names it. The engine declares; the
  game answers.

  ===========================================================================
  THE VERBS ARE THE ENGINE'S; THE WORDS ARE THE GAME'S
  ===========================================================================
  Every on-click below is a HOST verb registered by InstallMenuUIContent
  (Engine/src/ui/MenuUIContent.h) onto UIWorld's SHARED data source — the one
  named `scene`, which every document in the process binds against. That is why
  this markup, in a completely different asset root from the engine's menu, can
  drive the same volume slider and enter the same game mode with no C++ of its
  own: the actions were never per-document.

  Two of those verbs are deliberately ABSENT here, and their absence is the
  content boundary showing up in a file rather than in a build script:

    menuNewGame    loads MenuUIHooks::playScenePath, which is the ENGINE's
                   403-entity backpack demo. It is the engine testing itself and
                   it has nothing to do with this game. The host leaves
                   playScenePath alone on purpose (PlayerMain.cpp), so the verb
                   still exists and still works — this menu simply does not
                   offer it.
    menuOpenSystem opens the demo's swap log and live-document counters. That is
                   an ENGINE diagnostic, and a shipped fighting game's title
                   screen is not where it belongs.

  ===========================================================================
  THE SKELETON IS THE ENGINE MENU'S, AND ON PURPOSE
  ===========================================================================
  Header, stage, footer; the stage takes flex-grow; the brand and the status
  line live OUTSIDE the swapping middle. Every one of those is a fix for a
  measured bug in the engine's own menu (the brand vanished whenever a panel
  opened; setStatus fired from inside panels onto a label that was hidden at
  that moment; panels of different heights moved the content top by 30-40px).
  Owning the front end means owning the WORDS and the LOOK. It does not mean
  re-learning the same three lessons.

  NAVIGATION is the same model too: focus-scope="true" confines navigation to a
  subtree while it is VISIBLE, on-back="..." is what B/Escape invoke while that
  scope is innermost, and `if=` reading an app bool is the single source of
  truth for both. Movement is geometric over the laid-out rects.

  TWO LAYOUT RULES THAT COST TIME TO REDISCOVER, restated because this file is
  the first one a second title will copy:
    `position: absolute` writes ALL FOUR insets — Style::Edges defaults every
    side to 0, so a lone `right:` right-aligns nothing. Both absolute elements
    here write all four as zero.
    `gap`, `flex-grow` and the four insets are NUMBERS and reject a px unit;
    width, height, border-radius and border-width are Lengths and require one.
-->
<UI name="titleMenu" data-source="scene">

  <!-- TWO LAYERS, NOT THREE. The engine's menu puts a flat 55% veil over its
       backdrop because that art is a placeholder standing in for whatever a
       user eventually drops there, and a contrast FLOOR is the only thing that
       survives an unknown image. This backdrop is known: it is authored dark,
       with its lights deliberately outside the centre third where the type
       lives (see gen_title_art.py). So the flat veil is gone and the scrim is
       DIRECTIONAL — heavy on the left under the wordmark and the verbs, clear
       on the right where the stage lighting should actually show.

       The scrim is a two-stop `background-gradient`, not the stretched PNG the
       engine demo uses. That asset predates the property existing; scrim_ramp's
       own docstring still says "there is no gradient PROPERTY in the stylesheet
       language", which stopped being true. Same picture, one fewer file. -->
  <Element name="backdrop" class="backdrop"/>
  <Element name="scrim" class="scrim"/>

  <Element name="frame" class="frame">

    <!-- ===================================================== the header == -->
    <!-- THE WORDMARK IS THE HERO and it is built out of TYPE, which is a
         constraint rather than a preference. font-scale multiplies the 48px
         baked atlas AND is multiplied by the surface's own scale factor before
         it gets there, so a wordmark authored large is resampled up and goes
         soft on every monitor bigger than the reference — the engine demo
         measured exactly that and had to shrink its studio name and grow its
         logo to fix it.

         So the drama comes from the LOCKUP instead of from the point size: an
         ember bar, a small muted first line, and one crisp 34px second line.
         Nothing here is rendered above the atlas size at 1080p. -->
    <Element name="header" class="header">
      <Element class="wordmark">
        <Element name="mark" class="mark"/>
        <Element class="wordmark-text">
          <Label name="titleTop" class="title-top" text="UNTITLED"/>
          <Label name="titleMain" class="title-main" text="FIGHTING GAME"/>
        </Element>
      </Element>
      <!-- Where you are. Two labels rather than one bound string: a {hole} is a
           PATH and there is no expression language to choose between them.
           The wrapper carries no class of its own — `.header`'s space-between
           is what puts it on the right, and a class with no rule behind it is a
           selector somebody later goes looking for. -->
      <Element name="crumb">
        <Label class="crumb-text" text="MAIN MENU" if="!panelOpen"/>
        <Label class="crumb-text" text="OPTIONS"   if="panelSettings"/>
      </Element>
    </Element>

    <!-- ====================================================== the stage == -->
    <Element name="stage" class="stage">

      <Element name="verbs" class="verbs" focus-scope="true" if="!panelOpen">

        <!-- THIS MENU IS STATIC, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.
             ============================================================
             The engine's own menu binds everything: `if="menuMode0"`,
             `text="{menuMode0Name}"`, four anonymous slots. It has to. It ships
             WITH THE ENGINE and must not name a game, so the only way a title
             can put a verb on it is for the host to publish one and the markup
             to render whatever arrives.

             THIS FILE HAS NO SUCH PROBLEM. It is the title's own front end, in
             the title's own directory, next to the title's own modes. It knows
             it has a training mode because the same title wrote
             ../../../Player/src/UntitledFighterModes.cpp. Asking a registry at
             runtime whether the mode exists is indirection that buys nothing:
             the answer is decided at compile time by a file three directories
             away that this one is shipped alongside.

             And it cost something real. The verb was bound `if="menuMode0"`,
             the flag did not reach the document, and the menu drew NO PLAYABLE
             MODES IN THIS BUILD on a build that contained the mode -- with the
             registry holding it, both hooks set and the flag true in the data
             source. A typed button cannot fail that way, because there is no
             wire to come loose.

             What stays bound is the ACTION. `menuEnterMode0` is the host's job:
             it enters the mode, reports a refusal on the status line, and is the
             one part of this that genuinely belongs to the engine.

             THE COUPLING TO KEEP IN MIND: slot 0 is the FIRST mode registered,
             and registration order is menu order (GameMode.h). Both halves are
             the title's, so if a mode is ever registered BEFORE training, this
             line is what changes with it. That is a one-line edit in a file the
             same author owns, which is a better trade than a runtime lookup
             that can silently answer "no". -->
        <Button name="training" class="verb"
                text="TRAINING" on-click="menuEnterMode0"/>

        <!-- REPLAY and VERSUS ARE NOT HERE, AND THAT IS THE SAME DECISION.
             They are named in UntitledFighterModes.cpp as planned and
             deliberately not stubbed, because a menu entry that enters an empty
             screen is worse than one that is absent.

             This file used to carry three bound slots waiting for them, plus a
             label for the case where nothing was registered at all. All four
             elements existed to render something that does not exist, and the
             empty-state label is what the author actually saw on a build that
             HAD the mode -- a screen that read NO PLAYABLE MODES IN THIS BUILD
             because a flag did not arrive.

             So they are gone. The day REPLAY is built, this file gains a line
             that says REPLAY, typed, next to the line that says TRAINING. That
             is a smaller edit than the comment explaining why it was not
             needed, and it cannot render the wrong thing in the meantime. -->

        <Element class="verb-rule"/>

        <!-- OPTIONS rather than SETTINGS: the engine demo's word for its own
             panel, changed here for no reason except that this is a game and
             that is what a game calls it. The ACTION keeps the engine's name,
             because the action is the engine's. -->
        <Button name="options" class="verb verb-minor" text="OPTIONS"
                on-click="menuOpenSettings"/>
        <!-- QUIT is the one verb here you cannot undo, and it should not look
             like the one that starts the game. It is separated and DESATURATED
             rather than reddened: red is this title's primary accent, so the
             engine demo's trick of colouring the dangerous verb red would have
             made QUIT the loudest thing on the screen. -->
        <Button name="quit" class="verb verb-minor verb-quit" text="QUIT"
                on-click="menuQuit"/>
      </Element>

      <!-- ONE ROW SHAPE for every option: [label] [control], same height, same
           label column. A settings list is scanned rather than read.

           THREE ROWS, AND PILOT IS NOT ONE OF THEM. The engine demo's fourth
           row is a name field two-way-bound to `playerName`, which exists to
           greet you from the sample HUD after NEW GAME. Nothing in this game
           reads it — the fight's HUD shows frame data, not a greeting — and a
           setting that visibly does nothing is worse than an absent one. The
           property is still published and still shared; this menu just has no
           use for it. -->
      <Element name="optionsPanel" class="panel" focus-scope="true"
               if="panelSettings" on-back="menuClosePanel">

        <Element class="opt">
          <Label class="opt-label" text="MASTER VOLUME"/>
          <!-- Two-way: the Slider writes menuVolume straight into the shared
               source and MenuUIPublishCounters follows it into the AudioWorld
               every frame, writing the SETTING to disk only once the value
               stops moving. Press anywhere to jump, then drag — the drag is a
               pointer capture, so it keeps tracking past either end.
               key-step/key-ramp are the digital grain: one tap is 1%, because a
               control that only moves in 5% jumps cannot be set to 43%. -->
          <Slider name="volume" class="slider" bind-value="menuVolume"
                  min="0" max="1" key-step="0.01" key-step-max="0.05"
                  key-ramp="10">
            <Element name="volumeFill" class="slider-fill"
                     bind="width: {menuVolume | percent}"/>
          </Slider>
          <Label class="opt-value" text="{menuVolumePct}%"/>
        </Element>

        <Element class="opt">
          <Label class="opt-label" text="QUALITY"/>
          <!-- No separate readout: the lit chip IS the value. -->
          <Element class="chips">
            <Button name="qLow" class="chip" classes="chip-on: {menuQLow}"
                    text="LOW" on-click="menuQualityLow"/>
            <Button name="qMed" class="chip" classes="chip-on: {menuQMed}"
                    text="MEDIUM" on-click="menuQualityMedium"/>
            <Button name="qHigh" class="chip" classes="chip-on: {menuQHigh}"
                    text="HIGH" on-click="menuQualityHigh"/>
          </Element>
        </Element>

        <!-- VSYNC EARNS ITS ROW IN A WAY IT DOES NOT IN THE ENGINE DEMO. This
             game's fight runs on a fixed tick and is judged in frames: the
             training mode's whole job is to make frame advantage readable, and
             a player chasing a one-frame link wants the presentation path they
             will actually play on. It is a display setting on the demo's menu
             and a gameplay setting on this one. -->
        <Element class="opt">
          <Label class="opt-label" text="VSYNC"/>
          <Element class="chips">
            <Button name="vsync" class="chip" classes="chip-on: {menuVSync}"
                    text="{menuVSyncName}" on-click="menuVSyncToggle"/>
          </Element>
        </Element>

      </Element>

    </Element>

    <!-- ===================================================== the footer == -->
    <Element name="footer" class="footer">
      <!-- Persistent, so a message written from inside the options panel is
           actually SEEN while that panel is open — which is when most of them
           are written. It also carries the failure of a mode that refused to
           start: MenuUIHooks::onEnterMode returns a message rather than
           throwing, precisely so it lands here and not in a console the shipped
           player does not have. -->
      <Label name="statusText" class="status" text="{menuStatus}"
             classes="status-bad: {menuStatusOk | not}" if="!menuLoading"/>
      <!-- The async swap's own line. While the next scene's models warm on
           worker threads NOTHING has been torn down, so this menu is still
           live, still animating, and still able to say how far along it is. -->
      <Label name="loadingText" class="status" if="menuLoading"
             text="LOADING  {menuLoadDone}/{menuLoadTotal}"/>
      <!-- The button legend, and the caps are BOUND rather than typed: UIWorld
           republishes them the moment the player's hands move — A/B/L STICK on
           a pad, ENTER/ESC/ARROWS on a keyboard. Telling a keyboard player to
           press A is a small lie that makes a menu feel ported rather than
           made, and it would be a particularly bad lie on a fighting game.

           BACK is hidden at the root, where nothing is open to back out of.
           That is not decoration: the verb column is a focus scope with no
           on-back, so the press genuinely goes nowhere. -->
      <Element class="legend">
        <Element class="key">
          <Label name="keyNav" class="keycap" text="{uiGlyphNav}"/>
          <Label class="keyname" text="MOVE"/>
        </Element>
        <Element class="key">
          <Label name="keySelect" class="keycap" text="{uiGlyphSelect}"/>
          <Label class="keyname" text="SELECT"/>
        </Element>
        <Element name="legendBack" class="key" if="panelOpen">
          <Label name="keyBack" class="keycap" text="{uiGlyphBack}"/>
          <Label class="keyname" text="BACK"/>
        </Element>
      </Element>
    </Element>

  </Element>
</UI>
